  "content": {
    "id": "v2-044f0-p28-practice-slot-5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$, specifying the domain of the inverse.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "v2-044f0-p28-practice-slot-5-slot-5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{2x-1}{x+4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{4x+1}{2-x}$, $x\\ne2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{2x-1}{x+4}\\\\x&=\\frac{2y-1}{y+4}\\\\x(y+4)&=2y-1\\\\xy+4x&=2y-1\\\\xy-2y&=-1-4x\\\\y(x-2)&=-(4x+1)\\\\y&=\\frac{4x+1}{2-x}\\\\f^{-1}(x)&=\\frac{4x+1}{2-x}\\end{align*}$$\nThe range of $f$ excludes $2$, so the inverse has domain $x\\ne2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-044f0-p28-practice-slot-5-slot-5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=3-\\sqrt{x+2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=(3-x)^2-2$, $x\\le3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=3-\\sqrt{x+2}\\\\x&=3-\\sqrt{y+2}\\\\\\sqrt{y+2}&=3-x\\\\y+2&=(3-x)^2\\\\y&=(3-x)^2-2\\\\f^{-1}(x)&=(3-x)^2-2\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\sqrt{x+2}\\ge0$, the range of $f$ is $y\\le3$. The inverse therefore has domain $x\\le3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
